[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"ORM/ODM in Node.js: Mongoose (MongoDB) and Sequelize (PostgreSQL, MySQL)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial explores Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) and Object-Document Mapping (ODM) in Node.js, focusing on Mongoose for MongoDB and Sequelize for PostgreSQL and MySQL. ORMs/ODMs provide an abstraction layer, allowing you to interact with databases using JavaScript objects instead of raw SQL or database-specific commands. This improves code readability, maintainability, and security."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What are ORM and ODM?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An ORM (Object-Relational Mapper) translates data between incompatible type systems using object-oriented programming languages. In essence, it lets you query and manipulate data from a database using an object-oriented paradigm. An ODM (Object-Document Mapper) does the same, but specifically for document databases like MongoDB."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Mongoose (MongoDB ODM)"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mongoose is a popular ODM for MongoDB in Node.js. It provides a schema-based solution to model your application data. Mongoose includes built-in type casting, validation, query building, middleware, and more."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Mongoose Example"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, install Mongoose:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"npm install mongoose"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Then, connect to your MongoDB database and define a schema:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"const mongoose = require('mongoose');

mongoose.connect('mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/mydatabase', { useNewUrlParser: true, useUnifiedTopology: true })
  .then(() => console.log('Connected to MongoDB'))
  .catch(err => console.error('MongoDB connection error:', err));

const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: String,
  email: {
    type: String,
    required: true,
    unique: true
  },
  age: Number
});

const User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, you can create, read, update, and delete (CRUD) documents using the `User` model:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Create a new user
const newUser = new User({ name: 'John Doe', email: 'john.doe@example.com', age: 30 });
newUser.save()
  .then(user => console.log('User created:', user))
  .catch(err => console.error('Error creating user:', err));

// Find all users
User.find()
  .then(users => console.log('Users:', users))
  .catch(err => console.error('Error finding users:', err));

// Find a user by email
User.findOne({ email: 'john.doe@example.com' })
  .then(user => console.log('Found user:', user))
  .catch(err => console.error('Error finding user:', err));

// Update a user's age
User.updateOne({ email: 'john.doe@example.com' }, { age: 31 })
  .then(result => console.log('User updated:', result))
  .catch(err => console.error('Error updating user:', err));

// Delete a user
User.deleteOne({ email: 'john.doe@example.com' })
  .then(result => console.log('User deleted:', result))
  .catch(err => console.error('Error deleting user:', err));"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Sequelize (PostgreSQL, MySQL ORM)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sequelize is a promise-based ORM for Node.js that supports PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, and MariaDB. It provides a robust set of features for defining models, associations, migrations, and querying your database."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Sequelize Example"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, install Sequelize and the appropriate database driver (e.g., `pg` for PostgreSQL, `mysql2` for MySQL):"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"npm install sequelize pg pg-hstore # For PostgreSQL
npm install sequelize mysql2 # For MySQL"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Then, configure Sequelize and define a model:"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"const { Sequelize, DataTypes } = require('sequelize');

// Option 1: Passing parameters separately (other dialects)
const sequelize = new Sequelize('mydatabase', 'user', 'password', {
  host: 'localhost',
  dialect: 'postgres' /* one of 'mysql' | 'postgres' | 'sqlite' | 'mariadb' | 'mssql' */
});

// Option 2: Passing a connection URI
// const sequelize = new Sequelize('postgres://user:password@host:port/database')

async function testConnection() {
  try {
    await sequelize.authenticate();
    console.log('Connection has been established successfully.');
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Unable to connect to the database:', error);
  }
}

testConnection();

const User = sequelize.define('User', {
  name: DataTypes.STRING,
  email: {
    type: DataTypes.STRING,
    allowNull: false,
    unique: true
  },
  age: DataTypes.INTEGER
}, {
  // Other model options go here
  tableName: 'users' // Optional: Manually specify the table name
});

// `sequelize.define` also returns the model
console.log(User === sequelize.models.User); // true"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, you can perform CRUD operations:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"// Create a new user
User.create({ name: 'Jane Doe', email: 'jane.doe@example.com', age: 25 })
  .then(jane => {
    console.log(jane.toJSON());
  });

// Find all users
User.findAll()
  .then(users => {
    users.forEach(user => console.log(user.toJSON()));
  });

// Find a user by email
User.findOne({ where: { email: 'jane.doe@example.com' } })
  .then(user => {
    console.log('Found user:', user.toJSON());
  });

// Update a user's age
User.update({ age: 26 }, { where: { email: 'jane.doe@example.com' } })
  .then(result => {
    console.log('User updated:', result);
  });

// Delete a user
User.destroy({ where: { email: 'jane.doe@example.com' } })
  .then(result => {
    console.log('User deleted:', result);
  });"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Remember to synchronize your models with the database:"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"// This creates the table if it doesn't exist (and does nothing if it already exists)
(async () => {
  await sequelize.sync();
  // Code here
})();"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mongoose and Sequelize simplify database interactions in Node.js by providing object-oriented abstractions. Choose the right tool based on your database (MongoDB for Mongoose, PostgreSQL/MySQL for Sequelize) and project requirements. Both libraries offer powerful features for data modeling, validation, and querying, improving developer productivity and code quality."}]
